<Base title="semdock & protein–ligand interactions" active="Research" description="The semdock virtual screening protocol connects raw chemical datasets to ligand preparation, docking and filtering."><article><header class="container page-intro"><a class="back" href={sitePath("/research/#protein-interactions")}>← Research</a><p class="eyebrow">Protein–small-molecule interactions · 2026</p><h1>From raw chemistry<br/><span>to docking candidates.</span></h1><p class="lead">semdock extends jamdock-suite to work with user-supplied chemical datasets.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2026.104822" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the protocol ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/elrando/semdock" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Source code ↗</a></div></header><div class="container project-layout"><aside><p class="eyebrow">In this project</p><a href="#question">Research question</a><a href="#workflow">The workflow</a><a href="#evaluation">Evaluation</a><a href="#figures">Figure gallery</a><p class="aside-note">STAR Protocols<br/>7(3), 104822 (2026)</p></aside><div class="article-body"><section id="question"><h2>Starting with the data you have.</h2><p>Screening projects often begin with chemical collections in different formats. semdock connects these starting points to a repeatable preparation and docking workflow.</p></section><section id="workflow"><h2>Prepare. Dock. Filter.</h2><p>The pipeline accepts SDF, SMILES, and CSV input, prepares docking-ready ligands, performs CPU-parallel docking, and filters results.</p><Figure source="semdock" src="semdock-abstract.webp" alt="Graphical abstract showing ligand preparation, receptor preparation, docking and filtering" caption="Graphical abstract. Overview of the semdock workflow."/></section><section id="evaluation"><h2>Evaluation across protein targets.</h2><p>The paper evaluates the workflow using a library of 3,230 FDA-approved compounds and multiple protein targets. Runtime comparisons and docking-pose comparisons document its performance.</p><p>Docking prioritizes candidates for further study; a predicted pose is not experimental confirmation of binding.</p><Figure source="semdock" src="semdock-figure-2.webp" alt="Runtime chart comparing parallelized and multithreaded execution for four targets" caption="Figure 2. Runtime comparison for the reported benchmark."/></section><section id="figures"><h2>The workflow and its outputs.</h2><div class="figure-grid"><Figure compact source="semdock" src="semdock-figure-1.webp" alt="Side-by-side workflows for jamdock-suite and semdock" caption="Figure 1. Workflow comparison."/><Figure compact source="semdock" src="semdock-figure-3.webp" alt="Docking results and molecular-pose comparisons across four targets" caption="Figure 3. Docking outputs and structural comparisons."/></div></section><div class="paper-callout"><p class="eyebrow">Associated publication</p><h3>An extension of jamdock-suite for virtual screening from raw chemical datasets</h3><p>Emon et al. · STAR Protocols · 2026</p><a class="text-link" href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13615501/">Read the open-access paper ↗</a></div></div></div></article></Base>
